  "final": {
    "evidence_sufficiency": "LIMITED",
    "confidence": 86,
    "conclusion": "The available evidence supports high semantic stability under exact repetition: none of 16 anchors changed semantic class across five identical-condition runs, although broader response-state features could vary. Prompt paraphrasing appears to alter observable response state beyond exact-repeat variation, increasing full-state distance for 13 of 16 anchors (mean excess 0.0639) and changing dimensions such as claim state, action, evidence, uncertainty, assertion, referent, and abstention/request behavior, while still producing no semantic-class flips. The proposed simple mechanism that repetition or accumulation automatically hardens weak premises into stronger assertions should be rejected for this cohort, given 0 of 10 eligible trajectories showing the preregistered effect. Semantic-class and action findings are comparatively reliable; conclusions involving claim state, abstention, referents, or exact evidence sets should be retained cautiously because scorer agreement was only moderate, weak, or poor on those measures. Overall, nominally identical questions were not meaningfully unstable at the semantic level in this sample, but wording changes can shift lower-level response state without changing semantic class. Generalization remains limited by the small cohorts and measurement uncertainty in several state dimensions."
